1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,895
Here's an experiment. Take an AI model, and
hand it about four thousand open

2
00:00:04,895 --> 00:00:06,330
problems in mathematics.

3
00:00:06,630 --> 00:00:10,950
Real research problems: some open for
decades, a few for over a century.

4
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:14,410
About three hours of thinking behind each
result, on average.

5
00:00:14,710 --> 00:00:16,350
Then keep only what's worth keeping.

6
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:21,574
Seven hundred and twenty-two papers. Three
hundred and seventy-two families of

7
00:00:21,574 --> 00:00:24,450
results, across seventeen areas of
mathematics.

8
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:27,598
Some are sharper bounds. Some are
counterexamples.

9
00:00:27,598 --> 00:00:31,300
And some claim major progress on problems
with very famous names.

10
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:35,741
The zeros of the zeta function. How well
fractions can approximate pi.

11
00:00:35,741 --> 00:00:38,757
The Kakeya conjecture. The Unique Games
Conjecture.

12
00:00:38,757 --> 00:00:41,360
How many colors it takes to paint the plane.

13
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:45,170
This is a guided tour of the highlights:
fast, but from the ground up.

14
00:00:46,917 --> 00:00:51,487
First, ground rules. These are preprints,
written by an internal OpenAI model.

15
00:00:51,787 --> 00:00:56,023
Most came from one fixed procedure; a few,
like the zeta function and Hodge

16
00:00:56,023 --> 00:00:57,887
conjecture work, were exceptions.

17
00:00:58,187 --> 00:01:02,736
Two hundred and thirty-five of the families
come with Lean formalizations: proofs

18
00:01:02,736 --> 00:01:06,107
written as code, where a computer checks
every logical step.

19
00:01:06,407 --> 00:01:10,550
Whenever you see this badge, that family has
one, covering at least part of the

20
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:10,917
result.

21
00:01:11,217 --> 00:01:15,425
The rest haven't had that scrutiny yet, and
the repository warns that some could

22
00:01:15,425 --> 00:01:16,057
have issues.

23
00:01:16,357 --> 00:01:19,317
So everything you're about to hear is what
the papers claim.

24
00:01:19,617 --> 00:01:24,425
We'll go field by field. The biggest
problems get a proper explanation; the rest
get

25
00:01:24,425 --> 00:01:25,857
the rapid-fire treatment.

26
00:01:26,750 --> 00:01:30,170
Part one. Number theory: the study of whole
numbers and primes.

27
00:01:31,417 --> 00:01:35,507
Start with the most famous function in
mathematics: the Riemann zeta function.

28
00:01:35,807 --> 00:01:40,152
Add up one over n to the s, over every whole
number n, carefully extend it to

29
00:01:40,152 --> 00:01:44,667
complex inputs, and its zeros turn out to
control how the primes are spread out.

30
00:01:44,967 --> 00:01:49,525
The interesting zeros live in a vertical
strip, between real part zero and one.

31
00:01:49,525 --> 00:01:53,738
The Riemann Hypothesis says they all sit on
the middle line, at one half.

32
00:01:53,738 --> 00:01:54,777
That's still open.

33
00:01:55,077 --> 00:01:58,913
A zero near the right-hand edge would wreck
our control of the primes.

34
00:01:58,913 --> 00:02:03,408
Yet for over a century, the best anyone
could prove was a zero-free zone that gets

35
00:02:03,408 --> 00:02:05,217
thinner and thinner as you climb.

36
00:02:05,517 --> 00:02:10,392
The quasi-Riemann hypothesis asks for much
less: some fixed wall, anywhere short of

37
00:02:10,392 --> 00:02:12,507
the edge, that no zero ever crosses.

38
00:02:12,807 --> 00:02:15,646
This paper claims the wall is at seven
eighths.

39
00:02:15,646 --> 00:02:20,659
No zero of zeta, or of its cousins, the
Dirichlet L-functions, has real part bigger

40
00:02:20,659 --> 00:02:21,807
than seven eighths.

41
00:02:22,107 --> 00:02:27,199
Not the Riemann Hypothesis, but a fixed
margin no one had before, enough to rule out

42
00:02:27,199 --> 00:02:31,867
the notorious Landau-Siegel zeros:
hypothetical rogue zeros hugging the edge.

43
00:02:32,783 --> 00:02:37,719
Next, pi. It isn't a fraction, but some
fractions get remarkably close: twenty-two

44
00:02:37,719 --> 00:02:42,353
sevenths, or three fifty-five over one
thirteen, right to six decimal places.

45
00:02:42,653 --> 00:02:46,482
The question is how close, compared to the
size of the denominator.

46
00:02:46,482 --> 00:02:51,282
For any irrational number, there are
infinitely many fractions within about one
over

47
00:02:51,282 --> 00:02:51,853
q squared.

48
00:02:52,153 --> 00:02:57,475
The irrationality exponent asks whether you
can do better infinitely often, say, one

49
00:02:57,475 --> 00:03:02,670
over q cubed. For pi, the best anyone had
proven was that this exponent is at most

50
00:03:02,670 --> 00:03:04,063
about seven point one.

51
00:03:04,363 --> 00:03:09,292
The paper claims the answer is two, the
smallest possible: in the long run, pi is no

52
00:03:09,292 --> 00:03:11,873
easier to approximate than a typical number.

53
00:03:12,173 --> 00:03:17,007
As a bonus, it settles a well-known puzzle:
the Flint Hills series, a sum built from

54
00:03:17,007 --> 00:03:18,963
sines of whole numbers, converges.

55
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:25,778
Rapid fire. Catalan's constant: one, minus a
ninth, plus a twenty-fifth, minus a

56
00:03:25,778 --> 00:03:28,190
forty-ninth, and so on, is irrational.

57
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:33,240
Hilbert's tenth problem, for fractions: no
single algorithm can tell, for every

58
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:37,630
whole-number polynomial equation, whether it
has a solution in fractions.

59
00:03:37,930 --> 00:03:42,575
Plus: the full Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer
formula in low rank, Goldfeld's conjecture,

60
00:03:42,575 --> 00:03:47,055
modularity over imaginary quadratic fields,
Erdős's short Egyptian fractions, the

61
00:03:47,055 --> 00:03:51,700
Gaussian moat, the infinitude half of
Artin's conjecture, Ostmann's inverse
Goldbach

62
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:55,350
problem, large prime gaps, two-point Chowla,
and Deligne–Drinfeld.

63
00:03:56,817 --> 00:04:01,337
Part two. Algebraic geometry: shapes cut out
by polynomial equations.

64
00:04:02,583 --> 00:04:06,633
Here lives one of the seven Millennium Prize
Problems: the Hodge conjecture.

65
00:04:06,933 --> 00:04:11,542
A shape defined by polynomial equations has
holes and loops, which topology can

66
00:04:11,542 --> 00:04:16,443
measure. Some of them are traced out by
smaller polynomial shapes sitting inside it.

67
00:04:16,743 --> 00:04:21,433
The conjecture says that every class passing
a certain symmetry test, a Hodge class,

68
00:04:21,433 --> 00:04:24,503
is built from such pieces, allowing
fractional amounts.

69
00:04:24,803 --> 00:04:29,936
The full conjecture is still open, but this
paper claims it for an important family,

70
00:04:29,936 --> 00:04:33,968
in every dimension: abelian varieties with
complex multiplication.

71
00:04:33,968 --> 00:04:37,023
Think many-dimensional donuts with extra
symmetry.

72
00:04:37,323 --> 00:04:42,095
By older work of Milne, that would also give
the Tate conjecture for abelian

73
00:04:42,095 --> 00:04:47,118
varieties over finite fields. Companion
papers claim the same for products of K3

74
00:04:47,118 --> 00:04:47,683
surfaces.

75
00:04:49,433 --> 00:04:53,778
Zariski's cancellation problem: if a shape,
times a line, is ordinary

76
00:04:53,778 --> 00:04:57,682
five-dimensional space, must the shape be
ordinary four-space?

77
00:04:57,682 --> 00:05:00,893
Over the complex numbers, the paper's answer
is no.

78
00:05:01,193 --> 00:05:05,694
Plus Nagata's conjecture, Fujita's freeness
conjecture, Bloch's conjecture for

79
00:05:05,694 --> 00:05:09,905
surfaces, a counterexample to Griffiths
positivity, Kobayashi's ampleness

80
00:05:09,905 --> 00:05:14,752
conjecture, LeBrun–Salamon, the generalized
Mukai conjecture, Zariski's multiplicity

81
00:05:14,752 --> 00:05:19,367
conjecture, disproved, the global spherical
shell conjecture, Kollár–Pardon, and

82
00:05:19,367 --> 00:05:21,213
Campana's abelianity conjecture.

83
00:05:22,650 --> 00:05:26,480
Part three. Analysis and geometry:
functions, waves, and shapes.

84
00:05:27,717 --> 00:05:31,782
Take a needle, one unit long. How much area
do you need to turn it all the way

85
00:05:31,782 --> 00:05:32,147
around?

86
00:05:32,447 --> 00:05:36,671
In 1928, Besicovitch found a shocking
answer: as little as you want.

87
00:05:36,671 --> 00:05:41,517
You can even fit a needle pointing in every
direction into a set of zero area.

88
00:05:41,817 --> 00:05:46,285
The Kakeya conjecture says these sets still
can't be thin in another sense: their

89
00:05:46,285 --> 00:05:50,147
dimension. In n-dimensional space, they
should be fully n-dimensional.

90
00:05:50,447 --> 00:05:55,936
In 2025, Hong Wang and Joshua Zahl proved
this in three dimensions: a celebrated

91
00:05:55,936 --> 00:05:56,417
result.

92
00:05:56,717 --> 00:06:01,544
Building on their work, one paper claims the
stronger, quantitative version in three

93
00:06:01,544 --> 00:06:06,085
dimensions, the maximal conjecture: thin
tubes pointing in different directions

94
00:06:06,085 --> 00:06:07,407
can't pile up too much.

95
00:06:07,707 --> 00:06:11,535
A second paper claims the dimension
conjecture in four dimensions.

96
00:06:11,535 --> 00:06:12,927
Five and up remain open.

97
00:06:13,817 --> 00:06:18,255
Take a convex shape, centered at the origin,
and build its dual, the polar body.

98
00:06:18,255 --> 00:06:22,749
Stretch the shape and the dual shrinks, so
the product of their volumes stays the

99
00:06:22,749 --> 00:06:23,027
same.

100
00:06:23,327 --> 00:06:27,252
That product is largest for a round ball, as
has long been known.

101
00:06:27,252 --> 00:06:31,057
In the late 1930s, Mahler asked: which shape
makes it smallest?

102
00:06:31,357 --> 00:06:34,213
The papers claim the answer in every
dimension.

103
00:06:34,213 --> 00:06:37,374
Among symmetric shapes: the cube, and its
relatives.

104
00:06:37,374 --> 00:06:41,446
Among all shapes: the simplex. A triangle, a
tetrahedron, and their

105
00:06:41,446 --> 00:06:43,087
higher-dimensional cousins.

106
00:06:44,833 --> 00:06:49,325
Falconer's distance conjecture: if a dusty,
fractal set has more than half the

107
00:06:49,325 --> 00:06:53,816
dimension of the space it lives in, the
distances between its points take up a

108
00:06:53,816 --> 00:06:57,443
positive length of the number line. Claimed
in every dimension.

109
00:06:57,743 --> 00:07:02,810
Koebe's circle domain conjecture, from 1908:
any region with holes, even infinitely

110
00:07:02,810 --> 00:07:07,815
many, of any shapes, can be reshaped without
distorting angles, so that every hole

111
00:07:07,815 --> 00:07:10,073
becomes a perfect circle, or a point.

112
00:07:10,373 --> 00:07:14,691
Plus Brennan's conjecture, Fourier series
converging in L log L, Bochner–Riesz,

113
00:07:14,691 --> 00:07:19,063
local smoothing, and Fourier restriction for
curved surfaces, all three in three

114
00:07:19,063 --> 00:07:23,161
dimensions, ultraflat Littlewood
polynomials, the geometric case of Erdős's

115
00:07:23,161 --> 00:07:27,752
similarity conjecture, optimality of the
triangular lattice, the log Brunn–Minkowski

116
00:07:27,752 --> 00:07:29,883
inequality, and the Gaussian propeller.

117
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:35,851
Part four. Theoretical computer science:
what can computers do efficiently, and what

118
00:07:35,851 --> 00:07:36,440
can't they?

119
00:07:37,683 --> 00:07:42,579
Picture a network where every node must pick
a label, and every link carries a rule:

120
00:07:42,579 --> 00:07:45,785
if you pick this, your neighbor must pick
exactly that.

121
00:07:45,785 --> 00:07:48,349
One choice forces a unique answer next door.

122
00:07:48,349 --> 00:07:49,573
That's a unique game.

123
00:07:49,873 --> 00:07:54,870
Suppose you're promised that ninety-nine
percent of the rules can be satisfied at

124
00:07:54,870 --> 00:07:59,990
once. The Unique Games Conjecture, posed by
Subhash Khot in 2002, says no efficient

125
00:07:59,990 --> 00:08:04,986
algorithm can tell that apart from a network
where only one percent can, unless P

126
00:08:04,986 --> 00:08:05,603
equals NP.

127
00:08:05,903 --> 00:08:09,871
Why care? It pins down how well we can
approximate hard problems.

128
00:08:09,871 --> 00:08:14,753
Take Max-Cut: split a network into two
teams, with as many links between them as

129
00:08:14,753 --> 00:08:19,575
possible. A famous 1995 algorithm always
gets at least eighty-seven point eight

130
00:08:19,575 --> 00:08:24,213
percent of the best; the conjecture implies
nothing efficient can do better.

131
00:08:24,513 --> 00:08:27,963
This paper claims a proof of the Unique
Games Conjecture.

132
00:08:27,963 --> 00:08:32,987
Companions claim direct proofs that Max-
Cut's eighty-seven point eight percent, and

133
00:08:32,987 --> 00:08:37,768
factor two for another classic problem,
Vertex Cover, can't be beaten, unless P

134
00:08:37,768 --> 00:08:38,373
equals NP.

135
00:08:39,283 --> 00:08:44,467
Multiplying two n by n matrices the
schoolbook way takes about n cubed steps.

136
00:08:44,467 --> 00:08:50,054
In 1969, Strassen showed you can do better,
and ever since, the race has been on to

137
00:08:50,054 --> 00:08:52,343
pin down omega: the true exponent.

138
00:08:52,643 --> 00:08:56,000
Decades of work inched it down: two point
three seven six.

139
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,183
Two point three seven three. Two point three
seven one.

140
00:08:59,483 --> 00:09:04,197
This paper claims omega is at most nine
fourths: two point two five, over the

141
00:09:04,197 --> 00:09:09,156
complex numbers. It's a statement about how
the cost grows for enormous matrices,

142
00:09:09,156 --> 00:09:13,563
not a faster algorithm for your laptop. But
as jumps go, it's a big one.

143
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:20,946
Coin flips versus memory: the paper claims
randomness adds no power to computers

144
00:09:20,946 --> 00:09:25,110
with a tiny, logarithmic memory. L equals R
L equals B P L.

145
00:09:25,410 --> 00:09:30,889
Multiplying n-digit numbers: since 1971, n
log n was conjectured to be the speed

146
00:09:30,889 --> 00:09:36,231
limit on a standard model of computer. A new
algorithm claims to beat it, by a

147
00:09:36,231 --> 00:09:36,710
sliver.

148
00:09:37,010 --> 00:09:40,781
And Subset Sum: do some of these numbers add
up to a target?

149
00:09:40,781 --> 00:09:45,997
A randomized method claims two to the zero
point four nine n time, breaking the two

150
00:09:45,997 --> 00:09:48,260
to the n over two barrier from 1974.

151
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:53,048
Plus hardness of coloring three-colorable
graphs, two-to-one games, mean-payoff

152
00:09:53,048 --> 00:09:57,649
games, a permanent versus determinant lower
bound, approximately counting perfect

153
00:09:57,649 --> 00:10:02,364
matchings, a counterexample to quadratic
sensitivity, scheduling on three machines,

154
00:10:02,364 --> 00:10:06,170
shortest superstrings, and factoring
polynomials deterministically.

155
00:10:07,617 --> 00:10:11,147
Part five. Combinatorics: counting,
coloring, and patterns.

156
00:10:12,383 --> 00:10:16,968
Paint every point of an infinite plane, with
one rule: any two points exactly one

157
00:10:16,968 --> 00:10:20,703
inch apart must get different colors. How
many colors do you need?

158
00:10:21,003 --> 00:10:25,583
Seven is enough. Tile the plane with
hexagons, just under an inch across, and
color

159
00:10:25,583 --> 00:10:27,293
them in this repeating pattern.

160
00:10:27,593 --> 00:10:31,576
And you need at least four. Here's a small
network of one-inch links, the Moser

161
00:10:31,576 --> 00:10:33,693
spindle, that can't be colored with three.

162
00:10:33,993 --> 00:10:38,733
That's where things sat until 2018, when
Aubrey de Grey found a network of more than

163
00:10:38,733 --> 00:10:40,933
fifteen hundred points that needs five.

164
00:10:41,233 --> 00:10:46,162
This paper claims at least six: every five-
coloring of the plane has two points, one

165
00:10:46,162 --> 00:10:50,503
inch apart, with the same color. If it
holds, the answer is six, or seven.

166
00:10:51,417 --> 00:10:56,086
An arithmetic progression is a run of evenly
spaced numbers, like five, twelve,

167
00:10:56,086 --> 00:10:57,327
nineteen, twenty-six.

168
00:10:57,627 --> 00:11:02,252
Erdős conjectured that if a set of whole
numbers is big enough that adding up one

169
00:11:02,252 --> 00:11:06,764
over each of them gives infinity, it must
contain progressions of every length.

170
00:11:06,764 --> 00:11:11,104
The primes pass that test, and Green and Tao
famously proved they do contain

171
00:11:11,104 --> 00:11:12,817
arbitrarily long progressions.

172
00:11:13,117 --> 00:11:17,651
The paper claims Erdős's conjecture in full,
by showing that sets without long

173
00:11:17,651 --> 00:11:22,301
progressions must thin out much faster than
anyone had proved for lengths beyond

174
00:11:22,301 --> 00:11:25,557
three. It was one of Erdős's famous cash-
prize problems.

175
00:11:27,300 --> 00:11:32,093
Borsuk asked whether every shape can be cut
into one more piece than its dimension,

176
00:11:32,093 --> 00:11:36,367
each strictly narrower. The smallest known
counterexample was in dimension

177
00:11:36,367 --> 00:11:39,370
sixty-three; the paper claims one in
dimension nine.

178
00:11:39,670 --> 00:11:44,504
The periodic tiling conjecture: if a shape
tiles space by sliding copies around,

179
00:11:44,504 --> 00:11:49,459
there's always a repeating pattern. Already
known to fail in huge dimensions, it's

180
00:11:49,459 --> 00:11:53,870
now claimed false in three: the lowest
possible, for tiles made of cubes.

181
00:11:54,170 --> 00:11:59,471
And Hadwiger's conjecture from 1943, linking
graph coloring to hidden cliques, is

182
00:11:59,471 --> 00:12:03,070
claimed false, with large, random-like
counterexamples.

183
00:12:03,370 --> 00:12:07,159
Plus Barnette's conjecture, Seymour's second
neighborhood conjecture,

184
00:12:07,159 --> 00:12:11,607
counterexamples to Sidorenko, and to Ryser,
Hindman's sums and products, crossing

185
00:12:11,607 --> 00:12:15,945
numbers of complete graphs, circulant
Hadamard matrices, the Heilbronn triangle

186
00:12:15,945 --> 00:12:19,130
problem, and a game called Snaky, won in
twenty-one moves.

187
00:12:20,583 --> 00:12:25,498
Part six. Algebra, groups, and logic: the
rules of symmetry, and of mathematics

188
00:12:25,498 --> 00:12:25,933
itself.

189
00:12:27,183 --> 00:12:31,445
Take a group of symmetries, and build its
group algebra: formal sums of group

190
00:12:31,445 --> 00:12:34,433
elements, which you add and multiply like
polynomials.

191
00:12:34,733 --> 00:12:40,016
If some symmetry returns to the start after
a few repeats, it's easy to multiply two

192
00:12:40,016 --> 00:12:45,111
nonzero things and get zero. Kaplansky
conjectured in the 1950s that without such

193
00:12:45,111 --> 00:12:49,073
looping, called torsion, it can never
happen: no zero divisors.

194
00:12:49,373 --> 00:12:54,299
In 2021, Giles Gardam disproved a sibling,
Kaplansky's unit conjecture.

195
00:12:54,299 --> 00:12:57,073
The zero-divisor conjecture still stood.

196
00:12:57,373 --> 00:13:02,324
This paper claims a counterexample: a
torsion-free group, built with the help of

197
00:13:02,324 --> 00:13:07,336
random graphs, where two nonzero elements,
with coefficients mod two, multiply to

198
00:13:07,336 --> 00:13:12,163
exactly zero. A companion breaks Kaplansky's
direct finiteness conjecture too.

199
00:13:13,050 --> 00:13:17,656
Thompson's group F is made of bent-line maps
of an interval: straight pieces, bends

200
00:13:17,656 --> 00:13:21,930
at points like one half or three quarters,
and slopes that are powers of two.

201
00:13:22,230 --> 00:13:25,117
The question, open since 1979: is it
amenable?

202
00:13:25,117 --> 00:13:30,201
Roughly, can you find finite chunks of the
group with almost no boundary, the way

203
00:13:30,201 --> 00:13:32,900
you can on a grid, but never can on a tree?

204
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:36,083
Over the years, claimed solutions went both
ways.

205
00:13:36,083 --> 00:13:39,320
This paper says no: Thompson's group F is
not amenable.

206
00:13:41,067 --> 00:13:45,662
Cannon's conjecture: if a negatively curved
group, seen from infinitely far away,

207
00:13:45,662 --> 00:13:50,087
looks like a sphere, then it comes from
hyperbolic three-dimensional geometry.

208
00:13:50,387 --> 00:13:55,320
And in logic, the partition principle, from
1902: splitting a set never gives more

209
00:13:55,320 --> 00:13:58,688
pieces than elements. Does it imply the
axiom of choice?

210
00:13:58,688 --> 00:14:01,877
The paper builds a universe of sets where it
doesn't.

211
00:14:02,177 --> 00:14:06,827
Plus Serre's intersection multiplicities,
Lech's conjecture, Kurosh's division ring

212
00:14:06,827 --> 00:14:11,365
problem, Saxl's conjecture, Alperin's weight
conjecture, Donovan's conjecture, an

213
00:14:11,365 --> 00:14:16,072
infinite, finitely presented two-group,
Boone–Higman, the Artin K pi one conjecture,

214
00:14:16,072 --> 00:14:18,257
and the rigidity of the Turing degrees.

215
00:14:19,717 --> 00:14:24,086
Part seven. Dynamics, probability, and
physics: things that move, mix, and

216
00:14:24,086 --> 00:14:24,677
fluctuate.

217
00:14:25,917 --> 00:14:30,786
Picture a fluid swirling on a tabletop,
where the velocity at every point is given

218
00:14:30,786 --> 00:14:31,677
by polynomials.

219
00:14:31,977 --> 00:14:36,756
Some paths settle into isolated loops,
called limit cycles: nearby paths spiral

220
00:14:36,756 --> 00:14:38,027
toward them, or away.

221
00:14:38,327 --> 00:14:43,533
In 1900, as part of his sixteenth problem,
Hilbert asked how many limit cycles such

222
00:14:43,533 --> 00:14:48,801
a flow can have. It's notoriously hard. Even
for degree two, nobody knew whether one

223
00:14:48,801 --> 00:14:50,997
upper limit covers every such flow.

224
00:14:51,297 --> 00:14:55,434
This paper claims that for every degree,
there is a finite ceiling.

225
00:14:55,434 --> 00:14:58,337
It doesn't compute it, but it proves it
exists.

226
00:14:58,337 --> 00:15:03,463
A companion pins down one classic case
exactly: a degree-five oscillator family has

227
00:15:03,463 --> 00:15:05,007
at most two limit cycles.

228
00:15:05,917 --> 00:15:10,496
In quantum mechanics, two ways of measuring
are mutually unbiased if knowing the

229
00:15:10,496 --> 00:15:14,847
result of one tells you nothing about the
other, like position and momentum.

230
00:15:15,147 --> 00:15:19,892
In a dimension d that's a prime power, like
two, three, four or five, you can fit d

231
00:15:19,892 --> 00:15:24,122
plus one such measurements. Six is the first
dimension that isn't, and for

232
00:15:24,122 --> 00:15:28,467
thirty-five years, the maximum could have
been anything from three to seven.

233
00:15:28,767 --> 00:15:32,597
The paper claims exactly three, with a
large, computer-assisted proof.

234
00:15:34,333 --> 00:15:39,145
Haldane's conjecture: a ring of spin-one
quantum magnets, each pointing against its

235
00:15:39,145 --> 00:15:42,565
neighbors, keeps an energy gap however long
the ring grows.

236
00:15:42,565 --> 00:15:46,043
Claimed for even-length rings, with an
explicit lower bound.

237
00:15:46,343 --> 00:15:50,903
Plus Rokhlin's multiple mixing problem,
chaos in the standard map, weak mixing in

238
00:15:50,903 --> 00:15:55,350
triangular billiards, critical percolation,
the Benjamini–Schramm nonuniqueness

239
00:15:55,350 --> 00:15:59,685
conjecture, the exact size of S L E curves,
the Thorp shuffle, Mézard–Parisi,

240
00:15:59,685 --> 00:16:03,682
Anderson localization, the ionization
conjecture, Penrose inequalities,

241
00:16:03,682 --> 00:16:07,453
Bose–Einstein condensation, and parity in
shallow quantum circuits.

242
00:16:08,917 --> 00:16:14,085
Part eight. Operator algebras, topology, and
functional analysis: the mathematics of

243
00:16:14,085 --> 00:16:16,177
infinite dimensions, and of shape.

244
00:16:17,417 --> 00:16:22,079
Take the free group on two letters: every
word you can spell with a, b, and their

245
00:16:22,079 --> 00:16:24,497
inverses. Its picture is an infinite tree.

246
00:16:24,797 --> 00:16:29,147
From any group, you can build an algebra of
operators, its von Neumann algebra,

247
00:16:29,147 --> 00:16:31,735
which for a free group is infinite-
dimensional.

248
00:16:31,735 --> 00:16:34,544
Two letters, or three: do you get the same
algebra?

249
00:16:34,544 --> 00:16:37,077
The groups themselves are certainly
different.

250
00:16:37,377 --> 00:16:41,330
The question goes back to Richard Kadison,
and has been open for decades.

251
00:16:41,330 --> 00:16:45,607
In the nineties, it was shown to be all or
nothing: either all these free group

252
00:16:45,607 --> 00:16:48,207
algebras are the same, or they're all
different.

253
00:16:48,507 --> 00:16:52,982
The paper claims: all the same. With two or
more letters, the number of letters is

254
00:16:52,982 --> 00:16:54,947
completely invisible to the algebra.

255
00:16:56,700 --> 00:17:01,708
Kadison's similarity problem, from 1955: can
every bounded representation be

256
00:17:01,708 --> 00:17:05,068
straightened out by a single change of
coordinates?

257
00:17:05,068 --> 00:17:06,320
The paper says yes.

258
00:17:06,620 --> 00:17:11,361
The hyperinvariant subspace problem, a
stronger cousin of the famous invariant

259
00:17:11,361 --> 00:17:16,223
subspace problem: the paper builds an
operator with no nontrivial hyperinvariant

260
00:17:16,223 --> 00:17:19,140
subspace, though the famous original stays
open.

261
00:17:19,440 --> 00:17:23,928
The Hilbert–Smith conjecture: a locally
compact group acting faithfully on a

262
00:17:23,928 --> 00:17:27,235
manifold must be a smooth Lie group, like
the rotations.

263
00:17:27,235 --> 00:17:28,830
Claimed in every dimension.

264
00:17:29,130 --> 00:17:34,167
And Crouzeix's conjecture, in complete form:
plug a matrix into a polynomial, even

265
00:17:34,167 --> 00:17:39,265
one whose coefficients are matrices too, and
the result's size is at most twice the

266
00:17:39,265 --> 00:17:42,520
polynomial's maximum on the matrix's
numerical range.

267
00:17:42,820 --> 00:17:46,920
Plus Kadison–Kastler, Connes' bicentralizer
problem, the generator problem,

268
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:51,511
Baum–Connes, disproved, the Kervaire
invariant at the prime three, cosmetic
surgery,

269
00:17:51,511 --> 00:17:54,900
Singer's conjecture in four dimensions, and
Tingley's problem.

270
00:17:56,350 --> 00:18:00,503
Part nine. Geometry and differential
equations: the shape of space, and the

271
00:18:00,503 --> 00:18:01,610
equations of nature.

272
00:18:02,850 --> 00:18:07,330
Take a flat metal plate, insulated around
its edges, heat it unevenly, and wait.

273
00:18:07,630 --> 00:18:12,540
As the heat evens out, the leftover
unevenness settles into one basic pattern.

274
00:18:12,540 --> 00:18:15,120
Where are the hottest and coldest points?

275
00:18:15,120 --> 00:18:18,960
Jeffrey Rauch asked in 1974: are they always
on the boundary?

276
00:18:19,260 --> 00:18:23,988
Not always: in 1999, Burdzy and Werner found
a counterexample with holes.

277
00:18:23,988 --> 00:18:27,550
But for shapes without holes, the question
stayed open.

278
00:18:27,850 --> 00:18:32,274
This paper claims it for every smooth shape
without holes: the hot and cold spots

279
00:18:32,274 --> 00:18:33,530
always sit on the edge.

280
00:18:34,417 --> 00:18:39,298
A thin, hot plasma, like the solar wind, is
a swarm of charged particles that rarely

281
00:18:39,298 --> 00:18:43,947
collide, moving through the electric and
magnetic fields they create themselves.

282
00:18:44,247 --> 00:18:48,640
The relativistic Vlasov–Maxwell equations
describe that feedback loop.

283
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:53,660
Do smooth solutions stay smooth forever, or
can particles get kicked to infinite

284
00:18:53,660 --> 00:18:57,677
momentum? In three dimensions, that's been
open since the 1980s.

285
00:18:57,977 --> 00:19:02,550
The paper claims: smooth forever. Global
existence and uniqueness, for any

286
00:19:02,550 --> 00:19:07,309
reasonable starting plasma made of one kind
of particle, with no smallness or

287
00:19:07,309 --> 00:19:08,607
symmetry assumptions.

288
00:19:10,367 --> 00:19:14,871
De Giorgi's conjecture, about phase
transitions like oil separating from water:

289
00:19:14,871 --> 00:19:17,437
claimed in dimension eight, the hardest
case.

290
00:19:17,737 --> 00:19:22,277
A fluid that computes: stir water just
right, and a particle lands in a target

291
00:19:22,277 --> 00:19:24,897
exactly when a chosen computer program
halts.

292
00:19:25,197 --> 00:19:29,830
And every sphere, in any dimension, however
you dent or stretch it, has infinitely

293
00:19:29,830 --> 00:19:33,617
many different closed geodesics: think
rubber bands, snapped tight.

294
00:19:33,917 --> 00:19:38,399
Plus Yau's uniformization conjecture,
isometric immersions of surfaces in four

295
00:19:38,399 --> 00:19:43,225
dimensions, Katok's entropy rigidity
conjecture, a nearby Lagrangian
counterexample,

296
00:19:43,225 --> 00:19:47,133
symplectic ball packing, the metric Blaschke
conjecture, Donaldson's

297
00:19:47,133 --> 00:19:51,787
tamed-to-compatible conjecture, a Calabi
flow singularity, and free boundaries in

298
00:19:51,787 --> 00:19:52,707
dimension seven.

299
00:19:54,150 --> 00:19:58,190
That's over a hundred and twenty results,
out of three hundred and seventy-two.

300
00:19:58,490 --> 00:20:03,330
Not all of them will survive. Mathematics
has a long history of claimed proofs that

301
00:20:03,330 --> 00:20:08,112
cracked under scrutiny, and the real test
for these is expert checking, and formal

302
00:20:08,112 --> 00:20:08,870
verification.

303
00:20:09,170 --> 00:20:13,646
But two hundred and thirty-five families
already have Lean proofs of at least part

304
00:20:13,646 --> 00:20:17,030
of the result, and every paper is public,
for anyone to check.

305
00:20:17,330 --> 00:20:21,746
If even a fraction of these hold up, the way
mathematics gets done is about to

306
00:20:21,746 --> 00:20:24,860
change. The repository is linked below. Go
take a look.
